011-portal-nowhere-ledger: make ledger_no()'s out-of-bounds read explicit.

A wished-for magic portal is a portal "to nowhere": maketrap() leaves
trap->dst at {-1,-1}. A monster stepping on it reaches
mlevel_tele_trap() -> migrate_to_level(mtmp, ledger_no(&tolevel), ...)
with dnum -1, and upstream ledger_no() reads
svd.dungeons[-1].ledger_start, out of bounds.

On the macOS recorder build the read is deterministic. svc (704 bytes,
one struct context_info) is laid out immediately before svd (both are
__DATA,__common; `nm -n`: _svc 0x...8fb8, _svd 0x...9278). sizeof
(dungeon) is 92 and ledger_start is at offset 84, so the read lands on
svc + 696 = the first four bytes of svc.context.jingle, the last tune the
hero improvised. If no tune was ever played, it reads 0 and the ledger is
-1, and C panics ("level number out of range"). After a tune it is some
large number, and the monster "disappears out of sight" to wherever that
ledger decodes.

This patch spells that read out, so the value no longer depends on the
compiler's struct layout. The JS port, whose layout differs (its dungeon
is 112 bytes), then computes the same thing. On the recorder build the
value and the behavior are unchanged; re-recording reproduces the old
sessions byte for byte. It is one line, so upstream line numbers (and
the RNG log's @file:line tags) are unchanged.

--- a/src/dungeon.c	2026-11-03 09:12:45
+++ b/src/dungeon.c	2026-11-03 09:12:45
@@ -1375,7 +1375,7 @@
 xint16
 ledger_no(d_level *lev)
 {
-    return (xint16) (lev->dlevel + svd.dungeons[lev->dnum].ledger_start);
+    return (xint16) (lev->dlevel + ((lev->dnum == -1) ? *(int *) (genericptr_t) svc.context.jingle /* patch 011 */ : svd.dungeons[lev->dnum].ledger_start));
 }
 
